This looks like mid-2008. Headline CPI at 4.9% YoY points to June 2008, when it was 5.0% (May was 4.2%). The fed funds rate near 2.0% fits June 2008. Unemployment at 5.5% fits May 2008 (5.4%) or June (5.6%). Payrolls averaging -63k over three months also fits. The 2-year yield at 2.64% and the 10-year at 4.07% match June 2008, as does the US equity drawdown of about 17.7%.

The 3-month change in headline CPI is +0.9, which fits June (about 4.0% in March to 5.0% in June). The one-month equity return of -1.2% is odd, though. S&P fell about 8% in June 2008 and was roughly flat in May, so May is a better match with a 1m return of +1.3%... that is still not -1.2%. Gold falling 2.8% fits June, when gold dropped from about 890 to 930... that is unclear. The 10-year at 4.07% fits May-end at 3.86% or June-end at 4.0%. I'll go with June 2008, with unemployment of 5.5% being close to 5.6%.

